EXT. SCHOOL BUS - RAILROAD CROSSING - DUSK

An old yellow bus sits dead on the tracks, hazards blinking. The engine ticks. A signal bell dings in the distance.

A STRANGER, 17, in a letterman jacket, leans out of the open door. Beside him, on the steps, sits LARS FAIRBANKS-OJO, 17, holding a trumpet case.

STRANGER
It won't start.

LARS
I noticed.

STRANGER
Do you know cars?

LARS
I know trumpets.

The Stranger laughs. Lars doesn't. The bell dings faster. Far away, a train horn.

STRANGER
Okay. We should get off.

LARS
I'm not leaving my trumpet.

STRANGER
It's a trumpet.

LARS
It was my grandfather's.

He looks down the track. The light grows. He looks at Lars.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
What's your name?

LARS
Lars.

STRANGER
Wendell. I mean, Wendell is my name. Wendell Dube.

LARS
(deadpan)
Hi, Wendell.

WENDELL
Hi.

The horn blares. Closer. Wendell grabs Lars's arm. Lars grabs the trumpet. Together they leap from the bus and roll down the gravel embankment.

Behind them, the train hits.

A burst of yellow. A shower of steel. Silence.

Lars sits up, trumpet case clutched to his chest. Wendell sits beside him, breathing hard.

LARS
Thanks.

WENDELL
Sure.

He opens the case. The trumpet gleams, unscratched. He lifts it, blows a single, clean, brassy note.

They stare at each other. And laugh.
